<article class="env-lemma" id="03C4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03C4">Lemma <span data-tag="03C4">10.83.2</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a faithfully flat ring map. Let $M$ be an $R$-module. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
if the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is of finite type, then $M$ is of finite type, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is of finite presentation, then $M$ is of finite presentation, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is flat, then $M$ is flat, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
add more here as needed. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $M \otimes _ R S$ is of finite type. Let $y_1, \ldots , y_ m$ be generators of $M \otimes _ R S$ over $S$. Write $y_ j = \sum x_ i \otimes f_ i$ for some $x_1, \ldots , x_ n \in M$. Then we see that the map $\varphi : R^{\oplus n} \to M$ has the property that $\varphi \otimes \text{id}_ S : S^{\oplus n} \to M \otimes _ R S$ is surjective. Since $R \to S$ is faithfully flat we see that $\varphi $ is surjective, and $M$ is finitely generated. 
    <p>
      Assume $M \otimes _ R S$ is of finite presentation. By (1) we see that $M$ is of finite type. Choose a surjection $R^{\oplus n} \to M$ and denote $K$ the kernel. As $R \to S$ is flat we see that $K \otimes _ R S$ is the kernel of the base change $S^{\oplus n} \to M \otimes _ R S$. As $M \otimes _ R S$ is of finite presentation we conclude that $K \otimes _ R S$ is of finite type. Hence by (1) we see that $K$ is of finite type and hence $M$ is of finite presentation. 
    <p>
      Part (3) is Lemma <a href="/tag/00HJ" data-tag="00HJ">10.39.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>